\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Joining two endpoint indices}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{Take two copies of a one-dimensional coefficient space,
one at each end of a link. A sign change of basis at only one
end changes a scalar product of the two field coordinates.
The link value also depends on the bases at its ends. A path
gives a product of such link maps, with a domain at its
starting vertex and a codomain at its last vertex.
How can this map turn the two field coordinates into a
scalar that is independent of both endpoint choices?}
